#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23838cb5-a920-5ae2-a465-df3ceb9f48fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=3\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567495} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9e34bc-ec2f-5a1a-b60d-d32b2ee171f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567497} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9be118-4d71-569a-9a04-173a284a464c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-6\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567499} {:answer/id #uuid "68bf9178-68f8-5cbe-b3c9-07f425688ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=9\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567503} {:answer/id #uuid "ac0b7cff-5767-558b-adec-aa5bbc9a8e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=3\\,k+3\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567516}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567503} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2e28d75-8355-5d0f-87f0-44def3f6f712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8dc95925-14a3-5392-a27d-bd4ecb6a5537" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e5a5575a4fbdbd2b997ef94b9b1de64b00139b51efd8023fb88dbde0550df921.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $k$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=3\\,k+3\\,i\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-6\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=3\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y,z)=9\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb27149-46ea-5b3f-bec8-e1262dd9f0cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=-y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567498} {:answer/id #uuid "74beee04-ac9f-5766-98aa-08c6615db3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567506} {:answer/id #uuid "751c5db3-48c9-5ea7-9f18-22d1816d87bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567507} {:answer/id #uuid "bf40e9e2-9ee5-5942-9fff-675d15c41337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=-k+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567520} {:answer/id #uuid "f28d282e-b56d-5a5f-a4fe-6b9f0d1c1449" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567526}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567507} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ff8b66f-fdae-5f13-8ab9-c53a9ed88f33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91064f7e-fd51-5ac4-8b75-a514f3121392" :question/problem "![](images/69/690db47f3c87c0374217af2eba3acb05f73181224f2694a71a46bc1f0d5ee417.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $j$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=-k+j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=-y\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-2\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7079eded-ee16-571a-acd2-de38ee1a40b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-3\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567504} {:answer/id #uuid "725595fc-87bd-5be9-b5b4-50ffd305b6b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=3\\,y\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567505} {:answer/id #uuid "790b6c60-402e-5182-bdad-cb13add99a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=6\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567508} {:answer/id #uuid "b50e3182-c5bd-5384-89cc-baa078de6556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567518} {:answer/id #uuid "d9eb40eb-1776-546b-82a6-3f75879e6d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=3\\,k-3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567524}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567508} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37c6eb1-3158-5bb8-af4b-b955b9168151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b770816a-10c5-54b9-8d1d-a300af00b662" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d7f1fd540ca123750d0e37b16325f8d8d3901d7443843145098ffd45b2b765f.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $z$-axis. So, the $j$ and $i$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=3\\,k-3\\,j\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $z$-direction, so the $k$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-3\\,k\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $k$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=3\\,y\\,k\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=6\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567537 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53ef06fb-a6a7-56ae-95d0-c00d50e5d9e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567547} {:answer/id #uuid "799a344c-8f9c-51d0-b18c-ce30a54b03bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567552} {:answer/id #uuid "d77c8830-610e-5053-8f02-60354af2e90f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567565} {:answer/id #uuid "f689a4a4-9109-5cb6-b3ec-ab1981ab6b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{u}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567569} {:answer/id #uuid "f8937536-e093-5d02-9d4d-13bfcc65b442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567570}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567570} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a98fdcf-b602-59eb-b7e9-258d4644a8d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0743f203-e5f1-55c9-bb93-20fa59e22713" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\\\ 2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=4 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}$ and $y(u,v)=2 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}&- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\\\2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}&2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4 \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 u \\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\\\&\\quad{}-\\left(- 4 u \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\cdot\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\right)\\\\&=8 u\\left(\\cos^2 v+\\sin^2 v\\right)\\\\&=8 u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0de39219-e859-54ef-9083-15f0b622e4ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 u^{2} - 4 v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567538} {:answer/id #uuid "31c1a893-ae2b-53cb-acd4-d27657eaf58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567544} {:answer/id #uuid "69820abb-00e0-5a39-bed4-a1f9c291fcd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32 u v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567550} {:answer/id #uuid "cce5039d-93d1-58bf-8093-59e13b098d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 u - 8 v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567562} {:answer/id #uuid "dec6255b-e3c4-576d-9d93-5736cc40ed51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2 u^{2} - 4 u v + 2 v^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567567}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567550} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d2896b3-c9f4-549a-a194-d60084aab0a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567543}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "14fb1fae-3946-507e-ae2c-d1fb9c251294" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2} \\\\ 2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2 u^{2} - 2 v^{2}$ and $y(u,v)=2 u^{2} + 2 v^{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4 u&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- 4 v\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=4 u&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=4 v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 u&- 4 v\\\\4 u&4 v\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4 u\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 v\\right)-\\left(- 4 v\\right)\\cdot\\left(4 u\\right)\\\\&=16 u v+16 u v\\\\&=32 u v.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00841591-ac97-5e95-98ea-9b458faeffb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567536} {:answer/id #uuid "2b311643-a5e6-5209-86c1-e4491ab790b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u + 5 v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567542} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3d3992-ad4f-52e0-b18f-8be7fee562d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567548} {:answer/id #uuid "895dcade-5f80-5822-a03f-df6cd917e6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567553} {:answer/id #uuid "997af689-1196-582a-b9fe-276176a63ea8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 u - v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567554}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567548} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c0e19e-a993-5ce0-bac5-858d4f50d5ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7300c0af-afce-51e9-b6de-206aba0a7211" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}4 u + 5 v \\\\ u + 2 v\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=4 u + 5 v$ and $y(u,v)=u + 2 v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=4&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=5\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=1&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}4&5\\\\1&2\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(4\\right)\\cdot\\left(2\\right)-\\left(5\\right)\\cdot\\left(1\\right)\\\\&=8-5\\\\&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2df2f8-4cdf-5260-a0bd-e5b467c9cea9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} + \\frac{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567546} {:answer/id #uuid "a0ac5211-74c9-5395-9ad6-f7e81eed0e0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567555} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ef25f1-d252-5312-8c3e-917a34988df2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567556} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1e28d5-d5fb-5884-b12f-91865e507066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(u \\right)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567560} {:answer/id #uuid "cece1b9f-0301-5a10-9402-68fdbb045277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\cos{\\left(v \\right)} + \\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567563}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567556} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa0ad215-90a0-5d3f-8a3f-fac82505cd04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567559}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a9beb24a-cece-5a1f-ae4d-495cb0dcf965" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)} \\\\ \\sin{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=\\cos{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}$ and $y(u,v)=\\sin{\\left(v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=\\frac{\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}\\\\\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=- \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}}{2 \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}}&- \\sin{\\left(v \\right)} \\tan{\\left(\\frac{u}{2} \\right)}\\\\0&\\cos{\\left(v \\right)}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(v \\right)}}{\\cos{\\left(u \\right)} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567561 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "428e496a-ec9f-504b-bc78-5bb94221ad29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{v}{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567545} {:answer/id #uuid "60c9cded-3ced-5120-82ec-af00cafb915a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{u}{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567549} {:answer/id #uuid "a7ea9eba-de1d-57e7-b49d-14b8fed30d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567557} {:answer/id #uuid "d7a64856-ec6d-5923-b8ce-fcdc5da89766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{u + v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567566} {:answer/id #uuid "e7af2106-d7ea-552f-8fce-ef53ea001d38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u + v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567568}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567557} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f95702f-e2c8-5f7b-a4c1-491d69df3610" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c31c6390-19d1-5eb7-b2be-14050846f3e1" :question/problem "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}3 u - 6 v \\\\ \\ln{\\left(u + v \\right)}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u\\\\v\\end{bmatrix}\\to\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v)\\\\y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}$, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=3 u - 6 v$ and $y(u,v)=\\ln{\\left(u + v \\right)}.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&=3&\\quad \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}&=-6\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&=\\frac{1}{u + v}&\\quad \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}&=\\frac{1}{u + v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial(x,y)}{\\partial(u,v)}&=\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial u}&\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\begin{vmatrix}3&-6\\\\\\frac{1}{u + v}&\\frac{1}{u + v}\\end{vmatrix}\\\\&=\\left(3\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{u + v}\\right)-\\left(-6\\right)\\cdot\\left(\\frac{1}{u + v}\\right)\\\\&=\\frac{9}{u + v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3175e7dd-0742-5b6c-8d83-2cc7ab3297c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567578} {:answer/id #uuid "33385351-38cd-5b18-a8b0-28b21906824c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567579} {:answer/id #uuid "978f8262-dce9-5a46-b7f4-0067058d6fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567587} {:answer/id #uuid "a37c9a42-cb09-5783-8e02-9aec707b285a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i-x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567590} {:answer/id #uuid "b8f78514-203f-5de4-bff4-f9d95557e3ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567592}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567590} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7d7a045-c34d-5577-875d-3092fd7aab66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567600}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "182196c2-5417-5877-96b3-e9b6f7eeccc3" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b88788a561e16e833af000ae7e67c3d9d5c67e41c1903e9898d0b8fce6ad1ab.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-i-j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=i-x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567581 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a05d14a-b985-5ae4-8a35-b07167a50dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567573} {:answer/id #uuid "0f531967-ea96-5a03-b26d-1661cf5f87b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-2\\,i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567575} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb615bd-bd71-5809-ad90-83645b31cd29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567596} {:answer/id #uuid "d157e512-7b86-5ca5-b9ce-16d94b38f212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=-i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567598} {:answer/id #uuid "f8cfb010-e550-54e5-a9cd-53d5ed7265e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567605} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eb4c7c2-5a40-5556-88b2-2ebb7f30a881" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567574}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "41dcb040-7505-51ef-98f5-1ee0ac4f39d6" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3ff28cc39cac2fa96b6b71452d5c665aef2f346c58de0b60079776cbfdd88ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is negative for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i-j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=-i+2\\,j,\\,H(x,y)=-2\\,i-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $K(x,y)=-i+x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "050c7162-c486-5290-a6a5-a47b67734107" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-x\\,i+2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567572} {:answer/id #uuid "39082fbc-4f71-5ac8-ba33-f3e745066e0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=i-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567580} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ce6d7b-8bd9-5593-81e3-970e0ac1ccb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=i+j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567591} {:answer/id #uuid "db836b58-d4ce-5a2e-b96d-2c698b33bb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567602} {:answer/id #uuid "f45ee17a-5f0a-592a-b9f8-c491be228108" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=i-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567572} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd8cf37c-77ef-5e1a-9505-417f4f2824de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75fb1ea5-7dda-58cc-87ac-9d86439a0497" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5fdc54682a00fe32687dcb860cb2932e42f583e639939c7d2047a047d077ee5.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The vertical component (i.e., the $j$ component) is positive for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nG(x,y)=i-j,\\,K(x,y)=i-y\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' horizontal components (i.e., the $i$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=i+j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y)=-x\\,i+2\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567595 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b61d5bc-452c-5899-a3e3-9d88a6ed900d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567577} {:answer/id #uuid "708a31fe-e623-5ad7-ac1f-88b4270b2eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567584} {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3082d-363b-5dae-8361-7e5f8614f5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-i-x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567589} {:answer/id #uuid "e9cdac0d-f1eb-5850-8d3f-61b785980bff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=-x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567603} {:answer/id #uuid "f921b601-fbd7-5629-b857-480fc6bd986c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567606}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567603} :answer-field/id #uuid "9723ec5d-2425-55c4-9ac8-35c7d6519ee1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2a6d7d4-c154-5235-afcf-244f272faa02" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cdfb039d8dba8a696b71e37c24cebfbacaf470ee412cb879a24148f6ff444a48.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i-x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-y\\,j,\\,K(x,y)=j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=-x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ac52c2e-3691-554c-95c2-b5dda32bc830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y)=x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567582} {:answer/id #uuid "9944b680-6416-5475-a755-7807809ac763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y)=-i+x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567588} {:answer/id #uuid "c1e6ab14-319c-57cf-a7da-a4920701b161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567594} {:answer/id #uuid "cc346498-1edc-5c81-a7cd-90a984d9b4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y)=-j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567597} {:answer/id #uuid "da582e24-047f-5bdc-8aed-aab85304ae5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567601}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567582} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bcfb06c-54ee-59f6-9f3f-19e0c11f563b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4da0d27-8116-5fec-a6d8-65abd56aad43" :question/problem "![](images/ba/ba6f411a8090ce56a1694ce5bb2272b9d40b4fb5c6df33f00c46f8a62d0aaf58.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- The horizontal component (i.e., the $i$ component) is zero for *every* vector in our vector field. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y)=-i+x\\,j\n$$\n\n- As we move away from the origin in the horizontal direction, our vectors' vertical components (i.e., the $j$ components) get longer. Therefore, we can rule out the following options:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=-y\\,j,\\,K(x,y)=-j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y)=x\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567685 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a4d7d5-a9c3-55b4-a14c-6f528ed3d880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567680} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2838ca-8839-5c6e-bce5-e33be0fa2203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{26}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567682} {:answer/id #uuid "a082a1eb-994b-53ec-9845-86a7e450fd94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{13}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567683} {:answer/id #uuid "b867b942-3cfc-5194-bd6e-d438cd7f39af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26}a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567684} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ab28f6-4393-5c32-aec3-124a318f279f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567686}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567684} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b13fbef-105b-57e3-b389-514e96d3cd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567681}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc73ac92-52d3-59d7-b727-aa664056a93d" :question/problem "![](images/19/1918cfc0e0cc83334a16c7ee544b7314a8d12054999ce7c175604a2a39eb63ac.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/50/50d98e45b9996b85e50191c2164348ef4492dd45623c5c072699f2dac2feb87e.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1$ and $BH=5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(1)^2+(5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1+25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $\\sqrt{26}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{26}}{26}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567688 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a5d579-28f7-5c3a-bae4-34a3b60b782a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "88" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567687} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa1060e-b12c-5c42-8c9b-70a4fb99ef58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567706} {:answer/id #uuid "7904ddde-ae48-5ea7-bbb5-034227b717cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567707} {:answer/id #uuid "99b10892-f927-5504-bc64-8e9df4a0f182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567711} {:answer/id #uuid "a71188cd-1f1b-5b16-96df-e5367a4bdc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567707} :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe324ba-b15a-5007-872b-f95954e2d9c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1875adba-7053-5484-a02b-09be4856284c" :question/problem "![](images/ad/ad06bc93ffe1e70bf9ca543588c14a1185f306ea19646559d11cc5adadb005bd.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/83/83bf612af65f14848f10293bea459e926cd8246c2c48ea223709becd7acc1b1b.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=2\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-2}^{2}2\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(8)\\int_{-2}^{2}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(8)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=32.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=32.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+2\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-2,2].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{-2}^{2}2\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(2)\\int_{-2}^{2}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(2)\\cdot 4\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(32)+2\\cdot(8)=80.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567690 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ce6bc02-2b26-52ed-a5b6-c5451d5e627d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567696} {:answer/id #uuid "565f4f19-cb42-5168-a5c8-821f548a4f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567701} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd1220a-9c6e-5a8c-9b77-29ee1ef16fb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567712} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c8c68c-73d6-57a5-8c7e-a4881de48f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567717} {:answer/id #uuid "ee3d8e78-493a-5b38-a8cc-d6882598009b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567701} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eca439d-92bd-5da2-859e-c056e470838b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "271c5cb8-ae0c-5ebe-8a47-24bf9e8f9336" :question/problem "![](images/cf/cfe74c0d060535a5df1354feb7c154867f0e791b99ec92a0fed842b30891b2f4.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i-2y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-2\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-2\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-2)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-2)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=-4.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(8)+2\\cdot(-4)=8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567699 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29317222-5421-5fb1-855f-51dbea40c582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567692} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd44aa4-ab19-57cf-aa33-f57f48204f4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567703} {:answer/id #uuid "7a3e81f8-ccd0-5462-894c-4476798605cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567708} {:answer/id #uuid "83534f48-aaa7-53c6-8684-21b4df04a74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567709} {:answer/id #uuid "bb372993-fea9-5efb-8d41-e0b17d301210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567716}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567709} :answer-field/id #uuid "50e28c7b-a833-557d-9ce8-934efb556723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567698}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "534159d4-d049-5eb0-8513-f9361771d7fd" :question/problem "![](images/16/1622bef33dbaa424dcd7e19db6df88d38b0b50aba8b22675cf17876633ab4b95.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-5\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-5\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-5\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-5)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-5)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=-10.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(-5x\\,\\mathbf i-4y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=-4\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=-4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(-4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=-8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=-8.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(-10)+2\\cdot(-8)=-36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567702 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22028119-b2ec-57ce-919b-5e88f9647f95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567689} {:answer/id #uuid "276bedee-6b57-59f7-a264-ef6f3c1349f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567691} {:answer/id #uuid "539f71af-00fc-58c1-9e76-7b200f794d69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "152" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567700} {:answer/id #uuid "85871b78-ebde-53c1-adbd-a8c47555a3ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567710} {:answer/id #uuid "a743014e-c238-5224-a57f-333ca42ab962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567710} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a605c28-2912-5d71-b9a4-5bbba20c6989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567694}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5790bd40-5942-526b-abb0-15a807831527" :question/problem "![](images/48/480f1467463e8003c8a862d20e4e9feca23f175be4381ef613985f49addec4b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $6$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/11/1159164af4a9cacd4e06e3953e6323a6fdfad709cb0e0f69b9377c7a8ec528ff.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=3\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=3\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=3\\int_{-3}^{3}3\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(9)\\int_{-3}^{3}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(9)\\cdot 6\\\\\n&=54.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=54.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(3x\\,\\mathbf i+y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+3\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-3,3].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{-3}^{3}3\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(3)\\int_{-3}^{3}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(3)\\cdot 6\\\\\n&=18.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=18.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(54)+2\\cdot(18)=144.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567704 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29aa80de-ed67-5757-9439-951a37d02cb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567693} {:answer/id #uuid "41c56241-8734-5e10-a3e3-3d0e8a9b77d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567697} {:answer/id #uuid "69756482-279c-5260-974b-f0c8b9ba14da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567705} {:answer/id #uuid "f45c1b31-560a-5474-ba2d-72f40fe3fc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "42" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567719} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb18d14-ca09-593d-9ab9-ed7e7e13cb0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186567720} :answer-field/id #uuid "30dfcea1-b9c5-58a6-8b6c-0ea054556a13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567695}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65b405dd-edab-550c-b332-dd32dfaaa6d5" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c03e6d49996327392dc747c513102a61fdb53603f7b8e3942499e8bf96aac401.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F=4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j$ and a square $C$ with side length $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that, due to symmetry, the flux is equal across the vertical sides, and similarly for the horizontal sides.*" :question/worked-solution "If $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ In this case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&\\quad+\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds+\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the sides $C_i$ and unit vectors $\\mathbf n_i$ are shown below.\n\n![](images/db/db373c1f110dae0f35d11c09099adc64a8aee266a2a65df5d68dd8faa673854a.png)\n\nWe compute this integral in two stages.\n\n- First, consider the side $C_1.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_1=\\mathbf i,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_1}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf i\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=1\\mathbf i+t\\,\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf j,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n4\\int_{C_1}x\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=4\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(4)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_3$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_1\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_3\\,\\mathrm ds=8.\n$$\n\n- Next, consider the side $C_2.$ We have that $\\mathbf n_2=\\mathbf j,$ and therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=\\int_{C_2}(4x\\,\\mathbf i+5y\\,\\mathbf j)\\cdot\\mathbf j\\,\\mathrm ds\\\\\n&=5\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a line integral with respect to arc length, which we can evaluate in the usual way. Parameterizing the side $C_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+1\\mathbf j,\\quad \\mathbf r'(t)=\\mathbf i,\\quad t\\in[-1,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5\\int_{C_2}y\\,\\mathrm ds\n&=5\\int_{-1}^{1}1\\,\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(5)\\int_{-1}^{1}\\mathrm dt\\\\\n&=(5)\\cdot 2\\\\\n&=10.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy symmetry, the flux integral across the side $C_4$ must be the same. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_2\\,\\mathrm ds=\\int_{C_4}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n_4\\,\\mathrm ds=10.\n$$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,\\mathrm ds=2\\cdot(8)+2\\cdot(10)=36.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572109 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a6a3e04-07dd-5e46-ac74-a50abff88f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571777} {:answer/id #uuid "9a640bc5-fe07-5407-ae3b-8c6cef3acf65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571806} {:answer/id #uuid "b62dfa05-6c38-5ac9-b766-251c1de1c15f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571858} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe39d07-065f-50d1-be1f-6581aaaea180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571940} {:answer/id #uuid "ee1f63f0-d8dc-5fec-a4d6-2f15fec3ad73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}m" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571858} :answer-field/id #uuid "4718ed5c-b264-549b-898b-519d897a84b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ea2b07d-a05f-5f3d-9418-d56ab52d7fcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-119791" :question/problem "The vector $m$ has magnitude $\\frac{7}{2}.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m.$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $m$, we divide $m$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{m}{|\\,m\\,|}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}m=\\frac{2}{7}m\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572119 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da18e66-19b8-54da-a811-7bff65f316a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571563} {:answer/id #uuid "39a84a6a-bccf-512e-adc7-a3b19071646f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{17}}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571642} {:answer/id #uuid "c168b279-f15c-5669-a575-1c75c357532e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571883} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e39e22-a209-599e-8108-7f028d51f9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{17}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571921} {:answer/id #uuid "d726cf39-50e8-5e85-a32e-868dc97d0467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{173}}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571926}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571563} :answer-field/id #uuid "85ffb2ee-f378-58a7-b51e-ef917e3a8dac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2f3e6ef6-a3b7-556b-876b-54d269e801f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-77227" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-west and $|\\,a\\,|=5$, while the vector $b$ is directed due south-west and $|\\,b\\,|=12.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$   \n\n![](images/15/153aff405a3ae0859ff74fcb98deb09e038db7b033cbe54223866c9fe1724fb1.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={5}^{2}+{12}^{2} \\\\  & =169\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{169}=13,\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572126 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0294dd84-5297-5c6e-8070-0e3be90f9787" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{a+b}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571540}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571540} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0b511d2-5952-5b68-a426-4cf5aaba9091" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "46e2433f-faec-5a20-b0d6-cabb5029813a" :question/math-academy-id "q-275632" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed horizontally to the right and has magnitude $\\sqrt{3}$, while the vector $b$ is directed vertically up and has magnitude $\\sqrt{6}.$ Find a unit vector $u$ that is parallel to  $a+b$ and has the same direction as $a+b.$ \n\nType $a$ and $b$ to represent the vectors $a$ and $b,$ respectively.  \n\n$u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$ \n\n![](images/b7/b7d45d7cd1ea2c61961da8279d3f8702d3e52092fc6aab75a94d3b5076928dba.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =3+6 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{9}=3,\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2057facb-9b8a-526c-a11c-3348083b8b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\overset{\\to }{ST}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571595} {:answer/id #uuid "7b810dad-7762-5fad-9f57-62184f819cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{ST}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571751} {:answer/id #uuid "863033f0-b097-59ec-83fe-30cc1e87a732" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\overset{\\to }{ST}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571769} {:answer/id #uuid "9adea805-868a-56ca-bb88-e78a3fc0bb38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6\\overset{\\to }{ST}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571807} {:answer/id #uuid "d19c6a17-6128-503a-91a5-8be8628fed91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\overset{\\to }{ST}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571914}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571769} :answer-field/id #uuid "b902b0a7-5e71-5e88-9ec6-9f63e578d722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "50b46995-90d3-558d-b802-06393c983f30" :question/math-academy-id "q-69977" :question/problem "The vector $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ has magnitude $6.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{ST}?$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{ST},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{ST}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{ST}}{|\\overset{\\to }{ST}|}=\\frac{1}{6}\\overset{\\to }{ST}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572138 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d977df-2058-5b96-8d7e-8459946ef00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{14}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571602} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4ca2cc-f364-53e7-b5d7-754b69c071d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{98}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571672} {:answer/id #uuid "926b8523-c3d4-5e8e-b28f-59156da36f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{2}}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571798} {:answer/id #uuid "c628653b-f32f-521c-a5ac-5e6e7ac0095c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{6}}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571891} {:answer/id #uuid "c76d32fd-0d07-5a6f-a42b-f98aee2a62ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}(a+b)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571798} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecbe05ce-5c99-5a55-b52c-2b92b42e1761" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572093}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "64f8dbbc-bd8d-5e6e-b9e2-32a2e83cd44b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77229" :question/problem "The vector $a$ is directed due north-east and $|\\,a\\,|=7$, while the vector $b$ is directed due north-west and $|\\,b\\,|=7.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $a+b.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $a+b$ goes from the tail of $a$ to the head of $b:$   \n\n![](images/94/9468530a650f0bee117a4254aa87b5954c7bbbe2684d95f9db3f9961026abcb2.png)\n\nTo find the unit vector corresponding to $a+b,$ we have to divide by the magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the magnitude, first notice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2}+{7}^{2} \\\\  & =98\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the magnitude is\n\n$$\n|a+b|=\\sqrt{98}=7\\sqrt{2},\n$$\n\nand the corresponding unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{a+b}{|a+b|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{2}}(a+b).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572156 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fdcd9ee-41aa-59b2-9679-818cb4084f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571565} {:answer/id #uuid "52dd74f0-57c8-5c05-a6c9-94f401433860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571690} {:answer/id #uuid "732d5801-52f2-5d98-acd2-59cdcfe05a95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571734} {:answer/id #uuid "8c59c84a-5a15-5e0e-a1f6-c99349e42bf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571780} {:answer/id #uuid "afa2cd68-68c4-597f-8279-d2ad3f802291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571843}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571780} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb846a7c-a839-56a8-a16b-0c31f3561ba1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9630409b-82f5-5e41-add3-624263d041a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-11377" :question/problem "The vector $b$ has magnitude $20.$ Find a unit vector that is parallel to $b.$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $b$, we divide $b$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{b}{|\\,b\\,|}=\\frac{1}{20}b\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d6bcf0a9-648c-5969-b70f-729de3275926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(p+q)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571924}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571924} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a9adad1-9941-5e52-a6d0-5821492aa5a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bf062e19-facf-5f56-9031-7ed68b8d30bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-275627" :question/problem "![](images/13/13fc8ff564a7162c556d050a10df6e8f23c0fca269e3b88168ca6681be3ad3a3.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Find a unit vector $u$ that is parallel to  $p+q$ and has the same direction as $p+q.$ \n\nType $p$ and $q$ to represent the vectors $p$ and $q,$ respectively.  \n\n$u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $p+q.$ According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $p+q$ goes from the tail of $p$ to the head of $q:$ \n\n![](images/68/68c14ce5ff66272986e958f09f30289d56519e90b34fae23362753f84c042a50.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=2$ and $BH=3.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|p+q| & =AB \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $p+q$ is $\\sqrt{13}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|p+q|}\\cdot (p+q) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}(p+q).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133b9a0b-07a3-5279-9f37-33049aaaa2e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}}p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571572} {:answer/id #uuid "b626256e-983a-5f2c-9230-3938cb23814d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571857} {:answer/id #uuid "b80ef2f2-4a9b-5e69-872c-2881d1a0e191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{29}p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571864} {:answer/id #uuid "c245d639-18f5-577d-a798-c53b48699ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571885} {:answer/id #uuid "f784eacd-53c7-568d-957e-7b132d8f2939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{29}p" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571964}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571885} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3de6837-e66c-5b9a-a142-8fdce8446005" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb1f02a3-6465-553f-a943-f356abc8fd01" :question/math-academy-id "q-119796" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa72f6ea8c52a1f141f8469c3f932937b8b957956dc478811910e77c83336a9c.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $p?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/4d/4d4d7faa0c0611c8ef891cda5fef3b75a61aa97e0879fac525f58f4ce263c937.png)\n\nIn $△AHB$, we have $∠AHB={90}^{∘}$, $AH=2$ and $BH=5.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =\\sqrt{A{H}^{2}+B{H}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+25} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{29}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $p$ is $\\sqrt{29}.$ Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =\\frac{1}{|\\,p\\,|}\\cdot p \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{29}}p.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186572196 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "052d695c-75c2-537a-b2dd-d40fb706a504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{UV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571549} {:answer/id #uuid "2b72b380-5234-5f6a-925b-f6a08d294796" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\overset{\\to }{UV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571611} {:answer/id #uuid "31d03316-8870-51ff-8acc-393b2704b5f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\overset{\\to }{UV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571623} {:answer/id #uuid "4d4e311b-d223-5cb9-82e6-b49ae0b379b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\overset{\\to }{UV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571678} {:answer/id #uuid "77bdcaf2-bbad-5bfa-a013-fc5cd78f7ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\overset{\\to }{UV}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571743} :answer-field/id #uuid "537d3cf5-0d6e-5a1b-96fe-52ba35118a33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186572020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ec3ac6e6-c786-5106-a626-b9bded90e31e" :question/math-academy-id "q-119788" :question/problem "The vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ has magnitude $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{UV}?$" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overset{\\to }{UV},$ we divide $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{UV}}{|\\overset{\\to }{UV}|}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}\\overset{\\to }{UV}=3\\overset{\\to }{UV}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573869 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "096a33d6-d118-5767-9aef-e8a314eac3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{1/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573435} {:answer/id #uuid "3070acd5-da6e-5912-9c2f-3fa61b9ecd1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573486} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0eed41-4001-5d69-afa8-86d50d932215" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{1/2}}({x}^{2}\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573665} {:answer/id #uuid "df98d7d6-f700-5365-a939-7b86231547af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573717} {:answer/id #uuid "fe18764d-76e8-51ba-bcfd-15ecbbf56867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573766}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573717} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccf85ad2-68ea-542a-9e96-be9081261312" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0d01726f-52af-5592-8eb9-d6abaee05cc0" :question/math-academy-id "q-127950" :question/problem "Find $∇f(x,y,z),$  given that $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{1/2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}_{z}(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\left(-\\frac{x}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)i+\\left(-\\frac{y}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)j+\\left(-\\frac{z}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}\\right)k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3/2}}(x\\,i+y\\,j+z\\,k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573871 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "191fd92c-d897-524f-85fa-cdc13fdab7ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,i+\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573447} {:answer/id #uuid "23f78021-8f94-5150-a450-38155414595c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}{e}^{x/y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x{y}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573469} {:answer/id #uuid "371110b6-46e3-596b-9575-499451fd3d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1-y}{{x}^{2}y}{e}^{x/y}\\,i+\\frac{x-1}{x{y}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573495} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd76aaa-4503-5af9-b4d2-4e88ce7a5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,i+\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573628} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ca5fae-3d83-517f-b574-e5688492df40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\,i+\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186573638} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b9ee866-f5ad-511c-b339-3c9eaf2191fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186573831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11d17df4-8621-5dfb-b614-0130a53b1042" :question/math-academy-id "q-127649" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y},$ find $∇f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{y}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y} \\\\ {f}_{y}(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x}{e}^{x/y}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}-\\frac{y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}){e}^{x/y} \\\\  & =\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\text{ }i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{y-x}{xy}{e}^{x/y}\\text{ }j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
